cartridge "MINE CANYON/J.KRUTCH/1986"

' MINE CANYON
' A ColecoVision game based on "Mine Canyon" by John Krutch, Ahoy!
' September 1986 (Commodore 64 machine language, MINE CANYON at $C000,
' 2,131 bytes, started by the one-line loader MINE CANYON BOOT; the
' author's name is in the program: "MINE CANYON JOHN KRUTCH 1986"). Made
' for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy
' Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' machine code, orig/minecanyon.dis), the game is built for the
' ColecoVision: a dark green canyon floor between scrolling rock walls,
' round red mines with fuses that glide up one pixel at a time, a small
' saucer and a yellow patrol ship as smooth sprites, sound effects.
'
' The game: you fly a saucer down a canyon sown with mines. Rows of mines
' rise from the bottom of the screen; each row has one gap. Steer through
' the gaps and keep away from the patrol ship that zigzags up the canyon.
' Each row of mines is worth 10 points. A mine or the ship destroys the
' saucer; the fourth loss ends the game.
'
' From the original (orig/): the field scrolls up smoothly and a new row
' of mines comes in at the bottom every 9 character rows; each row is a
' full line of mines with one gap 7 characters wide in one of four places
' (at the left edge, left of the middle, right of the middle, the right
' edge), picked at random but never the same place twice running; 10
' points when a row comes in; the saucer moves in four directions inside
' the canyon; the patrol ship (two sprites side by side) starts at the
' bottom left and zigzags: right across the canyon, up 24 pixels, left
' across, up 24, and so on, coming back in at the bottom after it leaves
' the top; touching a mine (sprite-to-background collision) or the ship
' (sprite-to-sprite) blows the saucer up, then the field is cleared and
' the ship and saucer start again; the fourth crash ends the game; the
' score shown at the end, then a press plays again.
'
' Liberties: the canyon is 28 columns of 14 mines, 16 pixels each, with a
' gap three mines wide (48 pixels for a 14-pixel saucer; the C64 had 56
' for about 20) at mines 0, 4, 7 or 11; a row every 80 pixels (the C64's
' 72); speeds set for a human player: the field rises at 1/2 pixel a
' frame and the ship moves 1, both getting a little faster every 8
' rows (up to 13/16 and 1 3/4) - the C64 ran both at a constant and much
' higher speed; the saucer moves 3 pixels a frame across and 2 up and
' down, and diagonally (the C64 moved diagonals across only); collisions
' are box tests on the drawn shapes; the score, best score and spare
' saucers are shown during play (the C64 showed the score at the end);
' rock walls at the canyon's sides scroll with the field; the mines keep
' their colours (the C64 cycled their colour every interrupt, far faster
' than 3 Hz); GAME OVER then the title (the C64 asked PRESS THE FIRE
' BUTTON TO PLAY AGAIN); a title screen, credits and a title tune.
'
' Standing still or sweeping one way cannot last: every row's gap moves
' to one of the three other places, and the ship crosses every height.
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2): the stick flies the saucer. On the
' title, up and down choose, a button chooses.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given.
' Own xorshift random numbers.

